Two shapes the analyzers had the information for and were not reading.

OBJECTIVE-C. `for (NSString *p in paths)` declares the binding's class in exactly
the layout a local declaration uses — a direct `type_identifier` plus a
`pointer_declarator` — and `_objc_declared_receiver_types` never looked inside a
`for_statement`. The very next line's `NSFileManager *fm = ...` receiver was
already typed, so this was one node type missing from a walk rather than a
missing capability.

SWIFT. `if let x = <expr>` / `guard let x = <expr>` take their RHS's type through
the receiver-expression walker from #765. That also means `guard let task =
mutableState.tasks.last as? URLSessionDownloadTask` reads the CAST and refines
`task` from the collection annotation's `URLSessionTask` to what the code
asserts. PR #760's read-back had attributed part of VernissageServer's residual
losses to "guard-let-from-library bindings the file-wide leak matched by name" —
the old leaky scope map typed these by accident and correct scoping took it away.
This gives them back on a sound basis.

A BUG IN THE FIRST VERSION, AND WHERE IT WAS HIDING. Reusing the declaration
branch wholesale also accepted the loop's bare `identifier` child — which is the
ITERATED COLLECTION. So `for (NSString *path in paths)` typed `paths` itself as
`NSString`, and `[paths count]` re-keyed `NSArray -> NSString` on AFNetworking.
The churn reported LOSSES n=0 for that run: the defect sat entirely in the RE-KEY
column, which is where a wrong type hides, because the site still has an edge and
only its target is false. Only the declarator binds now, with a regression test
pinning both the binding and the collection.

MEASURED, every changed row read back:

  VernissageServer  228 changed  —  216 untyped -> typed, 9 hinted, 3 resolved
  SDWebImage          8 changed  —  8 gains (NSURL ×4, NSString ×2, UIColor, NSOperation)
  Alamofire          12 changed  —  4 gains, 4 resolved, 4 cast refinements
  AFNetworking        1 changed  —  1 gain

0 losses and 0 wrong re-keys on all four. io-boundaries unchanged (Alamofire
40 edges / 30 chains, VernissageServer 8 / 8, AFNetworking 22 / 22, SDWebImage
34 / 30) — the fourth consecutive item where filling the module slot buys
precision rather than recall.

STILL OPEN, FILED RATHER THAN GUESSED: swift `for s in xs` and
`xs.forEach { s in }`, which need a collection's ELEMENT type.
`_swift_bare_type` refuses `[T]` deliberately — a collection is not a receiver
type — so element extraction is a NEW rule and must not arrive as a loosening of
that one.

